capability idna/to-ascii  a host name written in ASCII for DNS and TLS, its labels above ASCII in Punycode after "xn--", by UTS #46's ToASCII
  heroes  url/idna.to_ascii
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  encodings.idna.ToASCII, str.encode("idna")
  note    UTS #46 for Unicode 17.0.0 (tr46-35), nontransitional by default, every one of its seven flags in `url/idna.Options`. The yardsticks: PyPI's `idna` (IDNA 2008 with UTS #46's mapping as an option, the fifth most downloaded project; not on this machine), Go's `golang.org/x/net/idna` (outside Go's standard library, which vendors it for `net/http`; run here as an oracle, v0.49.0, whose tables are Unicode 15.0.0) and Rust's crate `idna` (outside the standard library). Python's own codec is IDNA 2003 (RFC 3490, nameprep), and answers otherwise: "faß.de" is "fass.de" there and "xn--fa-hia.de" here (as Go's `Lookup`), "Bücher.DE" keeps "DE" there, "a_b.de" and "ab--c.de" pass there and are `invalid` here under `strict_options`, and "xn--a.de" passes there undecoded where its label decodes to U+0080, disallowed. Go's `Lookup` checks no length ("example.com." passes, here only under `url_options`) and reads "xn--a-.com" as "a.com", where UTS #46 refuses a Punycode label that writes nothing above ASCII (P4), as here. The mapped host is normalized to NFC by `unicode/normalize` (idna/nfc). The UTS #46 test file (IdnaTestV2.txt, 17.0.0) is the package's test: 6,389 of its 6,391 lines, the two left out holding a surrogate, which no `str` holds; ToUnicode, ToASCII and transitional ToASCII each, every check on, 19,167 operations, every one as the file says since 2026-11-20 (until then the 2,532 operations on 844 lines whose normalization the package could not make answered `unsupported`); X4_2 in ToUnicode read as no error, since ToUnicode reads no length

capability idna/to-unicode  a host name for a person to read, its Punycode labels decoded, by UTS #46's ToUnicode
  heroes  url/idna.to_unicode
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  encodings.idna.ToUnicode, bytes.decode("idna")
  note    UTS #46's ToUnicode always writes a text and flags an error beside it; here a host with an error fails `invalid`, the message naming the label and the rule, since a `T?` carries one or the other. Python's IDNA 2003 decoder refuses "xn--fa-hia.de", which does not round-trip under nameprep, where it is "faß.de" here and in Go

capability idna/options  how strict a conversion is: hyphens, bidi, joiners, the STD3 rules, transitional processing, DNS lengths, Punycode that does not decode
  heroes  url/idna.Options, url/idna.strict_options, url/idna.url_options
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    UTS #46's seven input flags, named as it names them. `strict_options` sets them as UTS #46's test file runs ToASCII; `url_options` as WHATWG's URL Standard's "domain to ASCII" does, not strict. Go's x/net offers profiles built of options (`idna.New`, `idna.Lookup`, `idna.Display`, `idna.Registration`, `idna.Punycode`, and the options `BidiRule`, `CheckHyphens`, `CheckJoiners`, `MapForLookup`, `StrictDomainName`, `Transitional`, `VerifyDNSLength`, `ValidateLabels`, `ValidateForRegistration`, `RemoveLeadingDots`); PyPI's `idna` takes `uts46`, `std3_rules` and `transitional` as arguments

capability idna/punycode  a text written in Punycode and read back, RFC 3492, without the "xn--" of a host's label
  heroes  url/idna.format_punycode, url/idna.parse_punycode
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  str.encode("punycode"), bytes.decode("punycode"), encodings.punycode.punycode_encode, encodings.punycode.punycode_decode
  note    RFC 3492's samples A to S, encoded as Python's codec encodes them (digits in lower case, where sample I of the RFC writes one capital as an annotation) and decoded from the RFC's own text. A "-" at the start, after no basic code point, is read as a digit and fails, as RFC 3492 § 6.2 and Go's x/net read it, where Python skips it ("-a" is U+0080 there); a number past the largest `i64` is `invalid` where Python, whose integers have no bound, reads on until the text ends; a code point past U+10FFFF or a surrogate is `invalid`, where Python decodes a surrogate. Both ways cost n log n of the code points, a Fenwick tree counting the smaller ones before each when encoding and settling the places last to first when decoding, where RFC 3492's loops, and Python's, read the text once for each different code point and insert each decoded one into a list; 30,000 code points of 20,000 values agree with Python's codec both ways

capability idna/nfc  the normalization to NFC that UTS #46's second step makes, and its first validity criterion
  heroes  url/idna.to_ascii, url/idna.to_unicode
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    `unicode/normalize.to_nfc` normalizes the mapped host, and `unicode/normalize.is_nfc` holds a label decoded from Punycode to V1, since 2026-11-20 (the coherence review's D17, `url/idna` moved from layer 1 to layer 2 to use it). Until then UAX #15's quick check stood in, and where it could not settle the question the call failed `unsupported`: 844 of the test file's 6,389 lines, 2,532 of its 19,167 operations, which now answer as the file says (694 lines refused in all three operations, 102 answered in all three, 30 answered by ToUnicode alone, 18 by transitional ToASCII alone). Python's IDNA 2003 codec normalizes by nameprep's NFKC, another step

capability idna/registration  IDNA 2008's own validity for a name being registered: RFC 5892's derived property (PVALID, CONTEXTO, DISALLOWED) and its CONTEXTO rules
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    UTS #46 is the processing for looking a name up; a registry holds a label to IDNA 2008's stricter table, which refuses what UTS #46's mapping table marks NV8 and XV8, and to the CONTEXTO rules of RFC 5892's Appendix A.3 to A.9 (the middle dot, the Greek keraia, Hebrew geresh and gershayim, the Katakana middle dot, the two kinds of Arabic-Indic digits), which read the script of a character, a table this package does not carry: `unicode/names.script` holds it, at layer 1, and this package stands at layer 2 since 2026-11-20 (the coherence review's D17), so nothing but the work keeps the block pending. PyPI's `idna` does it by default, Go's x/net in `Registration`

